ristian architecture, established in 401 CE during the reign of the Byzantine Empire. Its name, meaning 'not made by hands,' reflects its significance in the Christian tradition and the veneration of miraculous icons. The church was built under the auspices of local Christians who sought to create a sanctuary that embodied their faith and resilience during a period of increasing Byzantine consolidation and cultural flowering. As one of the few surviving structures from the early Byzantine period, it serves as a crucial link to understanding the architectural and religious developments of Southern Europe.

